   "id": "b038",
   "spec": "Reduce the variance of the LightGBM base learner by averaging several models trained with different seeds. In base_predict, replace the single lgb.train call with a loop over 3 seeds (seed, seed+1, seed+2), each with its own lgb_params(...) and bagging_fraction=0.8 and bagging_freq=1 added to the params so the models differ. Average the predictions on Xb and return the mean as p_lgb. This keeps the same stacking interface and only changes how p_lgb is produced.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Seed-bagged LightGBM for variance reduction"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 1,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b046",
   "spec": "logD depends strongly on ionization, so add a small block of substructure counts to the feature matrix. Define about 10 SMARTS patterns: carboxylic acid [CX3](=O)[OX2H1,OX1-], tetrazole, acyl sulfonamide, phenol, aliphatic primary/secondary/tertiary amines (non-amide, non-aniline N with sp3 carbons), amidine/guanidine, pyridine-type aromatic N, and quaternary N. In featurize (or a helper called from fit_predict), compute per-molecule counts with Chem.MolFromSmiles(s).GetSubstructMatches for each pattern and hstack them as float32 columns after the descriptor block. Keep the Tanimoto kernel on the ECFP bits only, as now, and make sure the base_predict slicing for descriptors (Xa[:, 2*N_BITS:]) is updated so the new columns are included in the RBF kernel and the tree models.",
   "status": "open",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Ionizable-group SMARTS count features"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
